    "result_text": "The build passes (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 the only concrete test class; `AcceptanceBase` is abstract and `AuditLogCapture` is a helper).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required every 400/409/429 rejection to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`. Several escalation handlers were still returning empty (`ResponseEntity<Void>`) or ad-hoc (`Map`) bodies.\n\nA `ProblemDetails.build(ex, status, detail)` helper already existed and produces exactly that shape (it's what the conforming handlers like `ValidationExceptionHandler` and `ForbiddenExceptionHandler` use). I routed the seven non-conforming handlers through it:\n\n- **400** \u2014 `MissingFieldsExceptionHandler` (was returning a plain `Map`; now problem+json, with the offending field names preserved as an `errors` property)\n- **409** \u2014 `CityAtCapacity`, `DuplicateEmail`, `DuplicateHousehold`, `DuplicateIdentity`, `DuplicateTelephone` handlers (were empty-body)\n- **429** \u2014 `DailyLimitExceededExceptionHandler` (was empty-body)\n\nHTTP status codes are unchanged in every case.\n\n**Keeping structural impact low:** each of these is a single-method handler class, so `WMC_other = 0` and `CC = 1` for every function I touched \u2014 the per-function cost stays at its floor. I made no change larger than swapping the response type and body construction (\u22483 changed lines each), and I reused the existing `ProblemDetails` helper rather than growing any class or introducing a shared abstraction that would have forced edits to the exception classes and YAML wiring (which would have increased the file-count multiplier, not reduced it).",
